# s6_key_window
#
# The dealer WRITES its key file and the online phase MAPS it, instead of the one buffer being
# allocated by the dealer and handed to the online phase. `patches/patch_key_window.py`: four edits,
# of which three are the mechanism and one is a cursor that hands mapped pages back to the kernel
# behind the read position.
#
# pool_attacked: host
# object_attacked: main
# cost_class: paid
# evidence: exact
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IT MEASURED
#
#   peak_host_kb      19,052,324 -> 18,916,820    -135,504 kB    -0.71%
#   W_host                 85.72 ->      85.10
#   wall                      27 ->          66 s
#   gate              COMM:1062390674 KEYS:18075947008, byte-identical to s5
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LEVER WORKS COMPLETELY AND THE PEAK BARELY MOVES, AND THE TRACE SAYS WHY
#
# The leader's poller, this run against `s5`'s at the same instants:
#
#            s5_keybuf_tight                s6_key_window
#   t=0.0s   VmRSS 18,918,876 kB            VmRSS 18,916,808 kB     the dealer's buffer, full
#   t=3.5s   18,918,876                      1,091,092             close() writes the file, frees
#   t=60s    19,052,052 (still rising)       ~1.1 to 1.9 GB        the whole online phase
#
# **17.8 GB of residency is removed for the entire 60 s of the online phase**, and VmHWM does not
# follow, because VmHWM is monotone and the DEALER's fill happens first. This is the same shape as
# MOAI-GPU's `before_attention` and SHAFT-CPU's `s7_embed_chunk`: a transient earlier in time than
# every lever aimed at it.
#
# It is published rather than filed off-path because it does reduce (-0.71% against a metric whose
# step-to-step resolution on this system is 0.01%, measured between `s0` and `s1`), because its gate
# is byte-identical, and because it is the half of the mechanism that the next step needs: a dealer
# that rewinds must have a file to rewind into, and an online phase that reads that file must map it.
#
# PAID, and expensively: 27 to 66 s. The 17 GB is written to a network filesystem and mapped back.
# There is no free fix for this object; the dealer must hold what it generates unless it puts it on
# disk, and disk is I/O.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE EXPLICIT RELEASE I WROTE WAS A DOUBLE FREE, AND REMOVING IT MADE THE STEP CLEARER
#
# Job 46262407: both parties died in 41 s at `gpu_mem.cu:91`, code 713
# `cudaErrorHostMemoryNotRegistered`, right after `close()` printed its SNNI_BUF lines. `close()`
# ends with `if (keyFile != "") { write; cpuFree(startPtr); }` and `cpuFree` is declared
# `void cpuFree(void *h_a, bool pinned = true)`, so the file-writing branch already frees AND
# unregisters. My extra call hit the same pointer one call later.
#
# **That failure is what the step's argument now rests on.** In the stock configuration that branch
# is dead (empty string), which is exactly why the program then hands the same buffer on:
#
#     sigma->keyBuf = sigmaKeygen->startPtr;
#
# Enabling the file removes the 17 GiB by itself. The build's guard therefore checks the PROPERTY
# rather than my mechanism: it fails if that hand-over survives the patch, because then the
# reservation and the mapping are resident at the same instant and the step measures nothing, which
# is how MOAI-GPU's `s7_fused_input_encrypt` removed 14,944 MiB for +0.04%.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS STEP DERIVES, and it is why the line does not end here
#
# The dealer only ever APPENDS: eight sites, either a generator handed `&keyBuf` that advances it or
# an explicit `keyBuf += n`, and `startPtr` is read only for `keySize = keyBuf - startPtr` and the
# one file write. So the dealer could write and rewind after each operation, and then its buffer
# needs to hold ONE operation instead of all of them.
#
# `p_keywindow_high` measured that, on this tree, without changing an allocation:
#
#     SNNI_KW|op_high=825125000|ops=97|keysize=18075946656
#
# **787 MiB against 16.83 GiB, a factor of 21.9 over 97 operations** (mean 178 MiB). That is the
# next step, and its size is now a measured quantity rather than a guess.
